  "aiSummary": "The Regulatory Reform Act of 2026 makes dozens of changes to North Carolina state laws to streamline regulations and reduce compliance burdens across energy, construction, environmental, licensing, and property development sectors. Key changes include reorganizing energy savings contract rules, lowering certain environmental fees, authorizing new construction contracting methods, modifying mining permit procedures, adjusting building code requirements, and establishing timelines for local government permit reviews.",
  "aiProArguments": "Supporters argue this bill reduces unnecessary red tape that delays projects and increases costs for businesses, developers, and property owners. They contend the changes modernize outdated regulations, speed up permit approvals through defined review timelines, lower fees for businesses like those operating underground storage tanks, and provide more flexibility in construction methods and materials. Supporters also say the bill helps military families by extending business reporting deadlines when members are deployed.",
  "aiConArguments": "Opponents raise concerns that lowering environmental standards (such as lead-dust hazard thresholds and energy efficiency requirements) could harm public health, especially for children and vulnerable populations. They argue that reducing mining permit review periods and weakening local government control over development may harm environmental protection and community input. Critics also worry that limiting local governments' transparency requirements and extending permit validity indefinitely could reduce accountability and undermine local planning priorities.",
